Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: "Who Taught the Canary to Play Bulldog?"
- Story & Context: Recorded during a bitter midnight strike at Grimethorpe Colliery, Terry Peel leaned his baritone sax directly into an open air intake while three pit-boys hammered an irregular 12-bar shuffle onto the ventilation line using heavy spanners. The recording caught a sudden, terrified flutter of wings from an overturned canary cage just before the intake valve backfired with a sickening subterranean groaning noise. Peel didn't stop blowing his overdriven blues horn; he simply leaned in harder, screaming through his reed until the cage foreman smashed his wrench against the pipe and bellowed: "Kick the iron till the dead birds crow!"
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: An antique wire canary cage lying crushed on damp coal rubble, lit by the harsh amber beam of a miner's headlamp, with ghostly brass musical notation floating like glowing yellow gas through cracked zinc ventilation tubes.

Track 2: Sister Martha's Five-Stone Altar at Barnsley Seam
- Story & Context: Deep in the flooded workings of Barnsley Seam, disgraced parish runner Sister Martha sat on an upturned grease drum, throwing five chipped carbon-slate pebbles across the sounding surface of an exposed air duct in a solitary, invented game of knucklebones. Between rounds, she drove an ungrounded 1964 Watkins Rapier guitar into howling, microphonic feedback against the shaking ventilation steel, weeping into the dark over stolen communion silver while Geoffrey’s baritone sax dragged a mournful, funeral-blues groan across her rusted slide notes. Her voice cracked into the open ductwork, shivering through two kilometers of stone: "I loved the pit more than the altar rail."
